WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.170
A drone can use G N S S position to stabilize over the ground,

00:00:05.170 --> 00:00:08.429
display location, navigate, record tracks, and support automated behavior.

00:00:08.429 --> 00:00:10.240
These capabilities make operation easier,

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:13.499
but they also create dependencies that operators must understand.

00:00:13.499 --> 00:00:18.207
Position hold compares the calculated location with the desired location and commands corrections.

00:00:18.207 --> 00:00:21.828
If the position solution becomes inaccurate, the aircraft may drift,

00:00:21.828 --> 00:00:23.639
correct in the wrong direction,

00:00:23.639 --> 00:00:27.623
or switch to a less automated mode depending on its design.

00:00:27.623 --> 00:00:31.606
Return-to-home relies on a recorded home point, current position, route assumptions,

00:00:31.606 --> 00:00:34.865
altitude strategy, navigation sensors, and an available recovery area.

00:00:34.865 --> 00:00:39.211
A valid-looking map icon does not guarantee that every assumption remains correct.

00:00:39.211 --> 00:00:43.919
Waypoint missions and geofencing depend on position and stored map or rule data.

00:00:43.919 --> 00:00:47.902
Navigation anomalies can disrupt a route, pause a mission, trigger failsafes,

00:00:47.902 --> 00:00:52.248
or place the displayed path at odds with what the crew sees.

00:00:52.248 --> 00:00:55.870
Flight logs, imagery metadata, and timestamps may inherit satellite-derived data.

00:00:55.870 --> 00:00:59.853
A navigation problem can therefore affect later mapping, event reconstruction, geotagging,

00:00:59.853 --> 00:01:02.750
or evidence even when the aircraft lands safely.

00:01:02.750 --> 00:01:06.009
Timing dependency can be less visible than position dependency.

00:01:06.009 --> 00:01:09.269
If a system uses satellite time to align records,

00:01:09.269 --> 00:01:15.063
a bad time solution may create misleading event order even when the map still appears reasonable.

00:01:15.063 --> 00:01:17.960
Operators should know which functions require satellite navigation,

00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:21.944
what degraded modes look like, how the aircraft signals reduced confidence,

00:01:21.944 --> 00:01:25.203
and which manual skills and visual references remain available.

00:01:25.203 --> 00:01:28.100
Automation supports judgment; it does not replace it.
